WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.170
If you don't care about your
privacy, that's your privilege.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.710
But as a society, we can't
afford to give up on privacy,

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.490
just like we can't afford to
give up on free speech. My name

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.880
is Arvind Narayanan. I'm a
professor of computer science at

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.380
Princeton University. One of the
areas that I study is AI. I've

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.070
also done a lot of research on
privacy. What connects these

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.380
topics is, I'm motivated by tech
accountability. Back in 2006,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.580
Netflix released the movie
viewing records of a half a

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.820
million of their customers. And
this was for the purpose of a

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.030
machine learning competition.
What they had said was that

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.330
because the customers had been
de-identified because their

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.840
names were not attached to those
movie viewing records, there was

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.930
nothing to be concerned about
from a privacy perspective. At

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.320
the time, I was doing my PhD.
And so my advisor, Vitaly

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.520
Shmatikov and I, we smelled a
rat. I wondered, could we cross

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.570
correlate people's reviews on
IMDB with the set of movies that

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.050
they had watched on Netflix. And
we were able to show that that

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.770
was possible in some cases. So
that's when I first started

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.330
recognizing that the assurances
that companies might give people

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.300
on privacy might not, in fact,
stand up to scrutiny. One of the

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.960
other areas where I was hearing
this a lot is, yeah, yeah, we're

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.360
tracking everything you're doing
on your apps, but it's all

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.410
anonymous. So we started to look
into that. And we just, you

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.710
know, we fell into this whole
minefield of, you know, the

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.880
really, really invasive, sneaky
tracking that's happening on our

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.410
devices. And no, it's not really
all anonymous, because it might

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.340
be attached to something like
your so called hashed email

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.190
address, and it can be used to
connect all of your profiles

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.670
from one device to another. And
so it can still be used to build

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.630
this one kind of uber profile of
you. And there are dozens of

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.920
companies that specialize in
doing that. One thing we didn't

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.640
expect, when we started doing
all this privacy research is

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.810
that it would be Silicon Valley
versus Silicon Valley. Today,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.040
probably the biggest protector
of privacy is Apple, perhaps

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.610
even more effective than a lot
of the privacy regulations

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.390
around the world, which are
certainly also important. A lot

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.290
of AI technologies today do have
powerful capabilities, but they

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.980
also have important limits. And
so we should be very careful

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:30.270
about relying on them. There are
many kinds of technologies that

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:34.680
are clubbed under this umbrella
term AI. Often, these

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.310
technologically have very little
to do with each other, except

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.650
that they're all forms of
machine learning, they learn

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.870
from data. And just because
technology is quickly moving in

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.070
one type of AI, most notably
generative AI recently, doesn't

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.610
mean that all of these other
applications of AI are getting

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.730
better or more accurate. It
doesn't mean necessarily that

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.730
they should be used at all. I've
had this suspicion for many,

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.540
many years now that a lot of the
AI technologies that are being

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.170
sold to predict people's future
behavior don't actually work.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.500
These kinds of algorithms are
used by banks for lending.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.410
They're used by insurance
companies. This is happening in

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.470
hiring when people apply for
jobs. This is happening even in

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.410
the criminal justice system,
when it comes to decisions about

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:23.040
bail and parole. When you look
at the accuracy of these models,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:28.680
it's barely better than random.
So is it ethical to make these

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.800
consequential decisions about
people when they're only

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.220
slightly more accurate than the
flip of a coin? That's one issue

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.220
with these things. There are
Well known racial biases and

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:44.100
other kinds of biases in these
models. As a kid, I thought of

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.430
technology as automatically a
force for good. But what I

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:51.390
started to realize growing up
that what tech does is it

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:55.290
amplifies both the best and the
worst of society. Machine

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:58.050
learning algorithms are
fundamentally about finding

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.440
patterns in a data set, and then
reflecting those patterns in

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.220
their outputs. So ultimately,
any of these datasets is

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.810
generated by people. And people
live in a society. And so there

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.990
are both individual biases and
the structural biases of society

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.880
itself, that are almost always
captured. When it comes to AI

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.000
and fairness, who gets to decide
is really the elephant in the

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.380
room. And I think that's perhaps
the most important question.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.700
Does the decision maker or the
developer of the algorithm, have

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.630
a process to get community input
from various stakeholders from

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:37.560
the people who are going to be
subjected to these decisions and

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.070
try to figure out what all the
different trade offs are? I

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:45.000
think even when a technology
gets very rapidly deployed, and

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.740
it's beneficial to those in
positions of power, when

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:54.000
everyday people feel that it's
unjust, they can and have been

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.900
able to successfully push back
and that should be front of mind

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:01.110
for us when we see dubious
applicantions of technology, we

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.220
shouldn't take it for granted

